WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 17 Student Video
Unit 5, Lesson 1: Miracles

1
00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:14.030
Some time ago, I decided to go visit a good friend of mine on a university campus.

2
00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:15.910
While I was there, I thought, you know what?

3
00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:19.470
I'm going to go catch up with another couple friends of mine who are atheists and

4
00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:24.090
see if we can have a good conversation about God, faith, and the big questions of

5
00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:25.470
life. And we had a great conversation.

6
00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:28.310
And I remember a question one of my friends asked me.

7
00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:31.490
He was actually a PhD student in physics.

8
00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:35.950
He just looked at me and he said, how can you possibly believe in miracles?

9
00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:40.990
I said well tell me why you ask what's your problem with miracles he said how can

10
00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:45.310
you believe in a miracle such as a resurrection that somebody can rise from the dead

11
00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:50.950
when science shows that people who die stay dead this student

12
00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:56.890
expressed a common belief that many skeptics have namely that somehow miracles

13
00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:02.730
violate the laws of science as popular as this objection may be it's based

14
00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:07.110
on a misunderstanding of the intersection between science and between faith.

15
00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:13.210
Here's why. Scientists aim to explain certain features of the natural world.

16
00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:18.790
They do this by observing some patterns and then drawing the most natural inference.

17
00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:22.250
In this case, when people die they stay dead.

18
00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:28.050
Death is final. But the resurrection is not a claim that Jesus

19
00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:33.970
rose naturally. The resurrection is a claim that Jesus rose supernaturally

20
00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:39.310
that God stepped into the world he stepped into history to perform a miracle

21
00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:44.970
namely the raising of Jesus from the dead which was the effect which could not have

22
00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:50.450
happened if the world was left to operate according to its normal processes alone

23
00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:56.230
this student was dismissing that any powers beyond the natural world could act

24
00:01:56.230 --> 00:02:01.990
within the natural world he limited explanations for things we see in the world

25
00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:05.510
to the regular patterns we observe around us.

26
00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:11.530
But this is a mistaken way of thinking and in fact it brings us quite obviously to

27
00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.190
the duck-billed platypus.

28
00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:20.030
At the end of the 18th century, the Western world first encountered the duck-billed

29
00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:25.230
platypus. It's a rabbit sized furry animal that lays eggs.

30
00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:28.490
It's got a duck bill, quite obviously because of its name.

31
00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:31.870
it has webbed feet and it's native to Australia.

32
00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:36.750
If you happen to see one be careful and watch out for its venomous claw.

33
00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:42.990
Back then Europeans were stunned even at the sight of a hide of a platypus.

34
00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:49.250
Many refused to believe that such an animal was possible but it wasn't until 1884

35
00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:55.070
when a platypus mother was shot and she had just given birth and then they

36
00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:57.510
found another egg inside of her.

37
00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:01.630
It was only at that point that some people began to believe.

38
00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:07.570
Why were so many people, including many scientists, hesitant to believe?

39
00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.890
The problem was not a lack of evidence.

40
00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:16.530
They had the fur and the testimony of their colleagues who had seen the platypus.

41
00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:21.470
You see, the problem was on the level of their worldview, their belief system about

42
00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:26.030
reality. You see, their worldview said, either you have mammals who give

43
00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:29.450
birth to live young or you have reptiles who lay eggs.

44
00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:34.050
It's not possible to have a mammal who lays eggs.

45
00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:38.630
And in this case when they were presented with evidence for that very thing because

46
00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:41.370
of their world view they dismissed it outright.

47
00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:44.850
This is how many people approach the miraculous.

48
00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:50.490
If they haven't seen it and it doesn't fit certain preconceived categories, then

49
00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:56.430
they dismiss it. It must be a hoax even when presented with evidence for

50
00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:01.430
miracles because of assumptions from their world view many people simply don't even

51
00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:06.250
consider that it could be true. And in fact they leave no room for the supernatural.

52
00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:12.710
That's why the question of the possibility of miracles really depends upon the

53
00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:18.230
existence of God. if God doesn't exist then of course miracles are impossible

54
00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:24.150
but if God does exist then miracles can happen and this puts a

55
00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:29.950
huge burden of proof upon the skeptic the skeptic actually has to show that

56
00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:35.370
God doesn't exist because if he even just possibly exists then the miraculous

57
00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:41.330
can happen so here's the bottom line just as the scientists in the day of the

58
00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:45.870
platypus needed to be willing to follow evidence when it took them outside of their

59
00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:50.750
preconceived ideas today skeptics need to be open to the evidence for the miraculous

60
00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:56.610
and to follow the evidence wherever it leads but not only are miracles possible

61
00:04:56.610 --> 00:05:01.950
we have good reason to believe that miracles actually happen today

62
00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:07.310
whenever I speak to Christian audiences I often ask a question I'll simply ask the

63
00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:12.450
question how many of you have seen or personally experienced the miraculous?

64
00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:17.030
And I don't mean like you're going shopping during Christmas season and you happen

65
00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:22.710
to get a lucky parking spot. I mean a supernatural miracle that can't be explained

66
00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:28.490
purely in natural terms. And every time I do it, dozens of

67
00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:34.410
hands go up. Friends, there are tons of living Christians today who believe

68
00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:39.790
they have seen and experienced miracles and are willing to testify to that fact

69
00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:45.750
in his massive academic study on miracles New Testament scholar

70
00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:50.390
Craig Keener notes that there are hundreds of millions of Christians today that's

71
00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:56.270
right hundreds of millions who believe they have personally experienced or witnessed

72
00:05:56.270 --> 00:06:01.670
miracles the deaf being able to hear the blind regaining sight

73
00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:07.650
and the lame being able to walk there are even cases reported of

74
00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:13.230
the dead coming back to life. Professor Keener carefully and meticulously

75
00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:17.190
documents these in his two-volume set on miracles.

76
00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:23.650
There's simply no good reason to dismiss the possibility of miracles before

77
00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:29.550
examining the evidence. And there is good reason to believe, as we have seen with so

78
00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:34.910
many testimonies, that miracles actually happen still today.

79
00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:38.110
Talking about miracles can raise a lot of questions.

80
00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:41.510
Why does God perform some miracles, but not others?

81
00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:47.150
Even in my own life, there's times that I've prayed and not seen the answer that I

82
00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:50.510
was praying for. And that can bring doubts and that can bring questions.

83
00:06:50.930 --> 00:06:55.250
But here's what I do know. I know that we worship a God who is powerful.

84
00:06:55.630 --> 00:07:01.350
And as Psalm 77 says, which you will see in this week's verse, we worship and follow

85
00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:03.710
a God who performs wonders today.

86
00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:06.810
This is a God who performs miracles.

